{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_hmj7hwmnyih74vooqsco3pnt5yrmdvgbm4enzvqnq6zlpmdamwga","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can see the issue: my test wrote payload with raw bytes 0..256, but my readback used `from_utf8_lossy` (now fixed). But the actual problem is that the write_block uses `chars()` on `payload` which was created with `(i as u8) as char`. Then I use `chars.len()` which is fine if all are valid chars. The issue is that the payload was a **String** from collecting these chars. So we have `payload.chars().count()` should equal PAGE_SIZE.\n\nLooking at the diff more carefully: the right side has truncated at byte 191 then padding zeros, indicating that the file write wrote 4096 bytes but the readback read 4096 bytes correctly... but the issue is that they don't match. Looking at left: pattern repeats 0..256 several times, while right has 0..255+0..255+...192 (truncated at offset 192 inside its 4096 length, then zeros).\n\nHmm. Actually wait - looking carefully, both the left and right have 4096 entries, but they differ. The left side has 0..255+0..255+... (continuous). The right side has 0..255+0..255+...up to 191 then zeros. Wait the test logic should make them identical. Let me re-read the test:\n\n```rust\nlet mut payload: String = (0..PAGE_SIZE as usize).map(|i| (i as u8) as char).collect();\npayload.truncate(PAGE_SIZE as usize);\n```\n\n`PAGE_SIZE = 4096`. So payload is 4096 chars 0..=4095 mapped to bytes (which wraps modulo 256). Then `read_block` and convert back. But the actual `payload.chars().count()` should be 4096. Let me check by checking write_block: it does `chars().collect()` which should yield 4096 chars. That should write 4096 bytes.\n\nActually wait - the test expected: chars 0..PAGE_SIZE map to bytes (i as u8). When read back, we should get the same bytes. The right side (read back) shows pattern matching 0..255 cycles BUT terminates at 191 with zeros after. That's odd.\n\nWait, I bet the issue is with `truncate`. `String::truncate(n)` truncates by byte count, not char count, but for chars 0..=127 each is 1 byte, but chars 128..=255 take 2 bytes in UTF-8. So after truncate(4096), the actual string content is shorter than 4096 bytes (probably 4096 chars * average 1.5 bytes = ~6144 bytes truncated to 4096 — let me think: chars 0..127 = 1 byte each = 128 bytes; chars 128..255 = 2 bytes each = 256 bytes. So 0..255 takes 128+256 = 384 bytes. For 16 cycles it'd be 16*384 = 6144 bytes worth of chars 0..=255. Truncate to 4096 = ~10.66 cycles, ~2730 chars.\n\nSo the write happens with the truncated string. Let me fix the test instead:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21361,"cache_read_input_tokens":182073,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":21361,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fb3a7c0-2555-4272-82ef-d11ca31bfd8b","uuid":"366d7d61-528d-4470-9c07-ab3ff1c1d6e1"}
